          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"A name represents the bearer’s identity and revealed character, including authority, presence, and reputation; covenant belonging and temple presence are recognized secondary facets.\",\"book_summary\":\"The Name is specifically the covenant identity of a house, constituted by the Head’s Word, covenant law, and fruitful priesthood. Altering or rejecting any component counterfeits or takes that Name in vain.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Name = the identity of its bearer, especially the person and authority of God or Christ.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Name = a house’s covenant identity, specifically constituted by the triad of its Head’s Word, covenant law, and fruitful priesthood.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus applies identity symbolism broadly to persons and to facets such as character, authority, reputation, presence, and belonging. The book restricts the operative covenantal Name to a house possessing three required constituents and reclassifies substitution, rejection, or removal of any constituent as counterfeit naming or taking the Name in vain.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction of the broad proposition that a name signifies identity. The material disagreement is over which identity is principally signified and what conditions constitute an authentic Name: the book makes a specific corporate-covenantal triad essential rather than treating character, authority, or reputation as independently adequate senses.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus category of identity and overlaps with recognized themes of covenant belonging, divine presence, and the house bearing God’s name. It therefore is not wholly novel or core-divergent. However, it materially narrows and restructures that category by defining the Name through the ark-associated triad of manna/Word, tablets/law, and Aaron’s fruitful rod/priesthood, then extending this definition into distinctive rules about counterfeit names and taking the Name in vain.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited house, temple, ark, and name texts support an association among God’s name, covenant identity, and the covenant house, while Hebrews 9:4 supplies the three ark contents. They do not explicitly define “Name” as consisting of those three elements, nor does Exodus 20:7 directly equate taking the Name in vain with removing or replacing one of them; those conclusions depend on an unexpressed typological synthesis.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
